    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/view/options/0/text",
      "before": "I: $y^2+10y+100$, $y\\ne10$\n\nII: $4x^2-6x+9$, $x\\ne-\\frac32$\n\nIII: $\\frac1{a-6}$, $a\\ne6$\n\nIV: $\\frac1{3b-4}$, $b\\ne\\frac43$",
      "after": "I: $y^2+10y+100$, $y\\ne10$\n\nII: $4x^2-6x+9$, $x\\ne-\\frac32$\n\nIII: $\\frac1{6-a}$, $a\\ne6$\n\nIV: $\\frac1{3b-4}$, $b\\ne\\frac43$"
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/view/options/3/text",
      "before": "I: $y^2-10y+100$, $y\\ne10$\n\nII: $\\frac{4x^2-6x+9}3$, $x\\ne-\\frac32$\n\nIII: $\\frac1{a-6}$, $a\\ne6$\n\nIV: $\\frac2{3b-4}$, $b\\ne\\frac43$",
      "after": "I: $y^2+10y+100$, $y\\ne10$\n\nII: $\\frac{4x^2-6x+9}3$, $x\\ne-\\frac32$\n\nIII: $\\frac1{a+6}$, $a\\ne6$\n\nIV: $\\frac2{3b-4}$, $b\\ne\\frac43$"
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/intent/source_solution",
      "before": "I cancels y-10 to y^2+10y+100 with y!=10. II has denominator 3(2x+3), giving (4x^2-6x+9)/3 with x!=-3/2. III cancels the positive quadratic to 1/(a-6), a!=6. IV cancels 9b^2+12b+16 from numerator 2 times that factor, giving 2/(3b-4), b!=4/3.",
      "after": "I cancels y-10 to y^2+10y+100 with y!=10. II has denominator 3(2x+3), giving (4x^2-6x+9)/3 with x!=-3/2. III has a^3-216=(a-6)(a^2+6a+36), with a^2+6a+36=(a+3)^2+27>0; canceling that quadratic gives 1/(a-6), a!=6. IV cancels 9b^2+12b+16 from numerator 2 times that factor, giving 2/(3b-4), b!=4/3."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/intent/unit_reason",
      "before": "A complete result/domain list unifies cancellation, remaining scalar factors, and removable exclusions without turning four denominators into independent clicks.",
      "after": "A complete result/domain list unifies cancellation, remaining scalar factors, and removable exclusions without turning four denominators into independent clicks. Row III now offers different first-factor orientations and signs to judge. D has the same correct I, II and IV rows as C and differs only in III, so those other rows cannot eliminate it. Whole-list selection still does not certify independently produced working or separate mastery of every row."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/key/rationales/0/derivation",
      "before": "II retains a denominator factor 3; IV retains a numerator factor 2. The displayed forms drop both scalar factors.",
      "after": "II retains a denominator factor 3; IV retains a numerator factor 2. The displayed forms drop both scalar factors. III reverses a-6 to 6-a without a compensating minus sign; at a=0 the original is -1/6 but the candidate is 1/6."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/key/rationales/0/misconception",
      "before": "Cancel the scalar multipliers together with the shared polynomial factor even though the scalars have no matching factor.",
      "after": "Cancel the scalar multipliers together with the shared polynomial factor even though the scalars have no matching factor. For III, falsely write a^3-216=(6-a)(a^2+6a+36) and cancel the quadratic, yielding 1/(6-a) instead of -1/(6-a)."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/key/rationales/3/derivation",
      "before": "The difference-of-cubes quotient in I has positive middle term 10y.",
      "after": "I, II and IV now match the correct complete option, including original domains. Only III is false: it requires first factor a-6, not a+6. At a=0 the original is -1/6 but the candidate is 1/6; the candidate also introduces an invalid pole at a=-6."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/8/key/rationales/3/misconception",
      "before": "Use the sum-of-cubes quadratic for y^3-1000 while retaining a difference first factor.",
      "after": "For III, falsely write a^3-216=(a+6)(a^2+6a+36), mixing a sum first factor with the difference quadratic, then cancel the quadratic to obtain 1/(a+6) while retaining the original exclusion a!=6."
    },
    {
